# Shell scripts are LF on every platform, including a Windows checkout (D-189).
#
# Measured 2026-09-20 on Windows 11 with `core.autocrlf=true`: the scripts are
# LF in the repository and CRLF in the working tree, and `shellcheck -S warning
# scripts/*.sh` — which D-175 wired into `make lint` — then reports **53**
# findings, **50** of them SC1017 "literal carriage return" plus three parses
# it confuses. Stripping the CR makes the same run completely clean, so the
# scripts themselves are fine and this is purely how they are spelled on disk.
#
# It is not only the linter. A `\r` at the end of a line becomes part of the
# last token for several `sh` builds, which is the D-155 class of failure one
# layer down — a harness that works on one platform and not the other for a
# reason nothing in it mentions.
#
# This is a no-op for macOS and Linux, and that is checkable rather than
# hoped for: every `.sh` blob in the repository is already LF
# (`git show HEAD:scripts/m2-gates.sh` is 1651 LF and 0 CRLF), and `eol=lf`
# only says "check it out the way it is stored".
*.sh text eol=lf
